{"page_content": "10 \n \n \n \nFigure 6. Backlit images of the droplet during hot and cool flame burning at atmospheric pressures of \napproximately 380, 570, 760, 1520, 2280 and 3800 mmHg.  The hot flame images are approximately 4 sec \nafter igniter withdraw and the cool flame images are approximately 5 sec after hot flame extinction.  The \ndroplet in the cool flame image at 1520 mmHg was close to the edge of the FOV.  The red circles during hot \nflame burning at the two highest pressures show the approximate location of the droplet surface. \n \n5.5 Summary of findings: \nCFI experiments has provided cool flame burning data for pure n-dodecane, and n-dodecane/iso-dodecane \nmixtures.  Farnesane, a commercially available renewable fuel characteristics were also studied.  The effect \nof pressure and helium dilution were studied for these fuels. Data for droplet regression rate, hot and cool \nflame diameters, extinction conditions, and radiant emission, both broad band and narrow band, are \nobtained and posted to the PSI website.   \n \n \nb. Benefits  \nLow temperature chemistry is of fundamental importance in the combustion of many practical fuels.  Low \ntemperature chemistry controls the autoignition of fuels when injected into a combustion chamber, and in \nhomogeneous mixtures of fuel and oxygen.  New internal combustion engines, such as the HCCI engines, \noperate at low temperatures reducing the pollution formation and increasing overall efficiencies. The CFI \nexperimental measurements provide a useful way to validate the complex chemical kinetic mechanisms that \nare being developed. The cool flame combustion process itself maybe of use in fuel reformulation \ntechnologies to produce hydrogen in fuel cell applications.  \nc. Patents   \nNone \n6.  Appendices \na. 30-Day Operational Accomplishments Report", "metadata": {"source": "CFI Science Final Report.pdf", "page": 11}}